#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5e58b3-f6d7-56e2-bccd-97095e94495e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Three Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "7bc66df5-26b7-5eb0-b756-f8b086607209" :question/math-academy-id "e-1520" :question/problem "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $8a$, $4c$ and $12c$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now match up the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(8a, 4c, 12c) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "872ca3cb-830c-5af5-92f1-230611a8a693" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when there are No Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "aa1c4cbd-2086-5634-809f-43e4dd6becf9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1603" :question/problem "What is the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $4a$ and $15b$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n15b &= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we try to pair off the individual factors, we can't find any common factors. The only common factor is one. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(4a, 15b) = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92176bb0-84dc-5fa4-8154-e974c05fff06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it is a Positive Constant"}] :question/id #uuid "6ce77198-a3b2-5e9b-98d5-db8922addc1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-816" :question/problem "Find the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2 \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2_(―) \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2_(―) \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair. Here, there is only one pair, so we just need to take that factor. There are no other factors to multiply it with.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(2x,10y) = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$ is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ac1e8c-128c-5c13-b845-105092ae74d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it Contains Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f4d78520-1050-5ca1-a2da-1b813269dd9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-817" :question/problem "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $16a$ and $4a$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a_(―) \\\\\n4a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot a_(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(16a, 4a) = \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{a} = 4a\n$$"}]}
